1. The plan: nothing for a later stop stands in an earlier stop's way out
==============================================================================
  status: feasible, left behind: 0
    stop 2  parcel#5             x=     0 mm  y=     0 mm
    stop 0  bakery-rack#1        x=     0 mm  y=  1200 mm
    stop 2  parcel#4             x=   400 mm  y=   600 mm
    stop 2  parcel#2             x=   800 mm  y=   600 mm
    stop 0  bakery-rack#2        x=   800 mm  y=  1200 mm
    stop 2  parcel#6             x=  1200 mm  y=   600 mm
    stop 2  dry-goods-pallet#1   x=  1600 mm  y=     0 mm
    stop 2  parcel#1             x=  2400 mm  y=     0 mm
    stop 1  drinks-pallet#1      x=  3000 mm  y=     0 mm
    stop 2  parcel#3             x=  3000 mm  y=   800 mm

  x is the distance from the bulkhead, so the rear doors are at x = 4200 mm. The
  bakery racks sit at the front, but the aisle beside them runs clear to the doors:
  the rule is that nothing due later blocks every way out, not that stop 0 is last in.

==============================================================================
2. Axle loads against their ratings
==============================================================================
  payload: 968 kg of 1300 kg allowed
  front axle: 1519.4 kg of 1650 kg
  rear axle:  1848.6 kg of 2200 kg

  the same load with a 1450 kg front axle rating:
    left behind: drinks-pallet#1, reason no_feasible_placement (observed)

  The axle rule refuses a placement rather than reporting an overload afterwards.
  Note the reason: an item the axles cannot carry is reported as the generic
  `no_feasible_placement`, not as an axle problem, so when a load comes up short on
  a vehicle with axle ratings, check the reactions before shopping for a bigger van.

